Zoho CRM's free edition allows more users and records than HubSpot's free plan, according to each vendor's published pages, but its limits are easy to misread because Zoho describes them on several pages that do not always agree. This guide pulls together what Zoho says the free edition includes, the numbers worth checking before you commit, what the first paid edition adds, and what upgrading costs per user. It is based on Zoho's own published pages, not on our own use of the product, so confirm anything important on the live page. For how it stacks up against other free plans, see our [free CRM comparison](/posts/free-crm-for-small-business-compared) and our guide to [HubSpot's free CRM limits](/posts/hubspot-free-crm-limits).

## What the free edition includes

Zoho's free edition guide says you get three users, 150 emails per day, storage for 5,000 contact records, no Zoho branding on your emails, five workflow automation rules and seven native integrations with other Zoho apps. Zoho's pricing page adds contact management, follow-up reminders, up to 10 custom email templates, tasks, meetings and calls, standard reports, mobile apps, free data import, and 5,000 API calls per day. The same page says you can export data up to 10 times for free.

The free page says there is no credit card requirement, no trial period and no forced upgrade. Zoho's help guide describes the free edition as supporting up to three users with basic CRM functionality, and its user-management article says the free edition allows up to 3 users.

![Line chart of Zoho CRM paid edition prices per user per month, billed monthly versus annually, from Standard at 14 to 20 dollars up to Ultimate at 52 to 65 dollars](/images/site1/zoho-crm-free-edition-limits-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from Zoho's published pricing pages.*

## The limits that matter

**Users.** Three. The number is stated consistently across Zoho's pages, so this is the cap you are most likely to meet first if you add a fourth person.

**Records.** Zoho's guide gives 5,000 contact records, and its comparison table lists 5,000 records for the free edition against 1,000 for HubSpot. The same guide's closing line, however, says there is no cap on contacts at 1,000 records, which reads like a leftover from an older version. We treat 5,000 as the working figure and suggest checking your own account's limit.

**Email.** The guide lists 150 emails per day. Zoho's Standard page lists email integration and mass emails as additions on top of Free, so check what the free edition's 150 daily emails cover before you plan a campaign around them.

**Automation.** Up to five workflow rules. Zoho's help-centre "Choose Your Path" guide marks workflows on the free edition as "with limited actions", so five rules does not mean five unrestricted automations.

**Features held back.** Zoho's "Choose Your Path" guide shows cadences, Blueprint process enforcement, and inventory management with quotes as paid-edition features. Its pricing page lists AI agents, predictive intelligence, process management and unlimited reports among the paid editions.

**Webforms.** The free guide says you get one webform per module with an auto-responder, while the Standard page lists data capture via forms as a Standard feature. These two descriptions do not obviously fit together, so test the form builder in a free account before you build a lead-capture process on it.

## What Standard adds

Zoho's Standard edition page lists sales forecasting, data enrichment, custom modules, email integration with mass emails, built-in calling and data capture via forms. The page's FAQ also names workflows, assignment rules, cadences, multiple pipelines, unlimited reports and Marketplace integrations as things Standard unlocks. Because the free edition also has five workflow rules, the Standard wording presumably means higher limits or more actions, but Zoho's pages do not spell that out.

Professional builds on that with Blueprint-style process management, inventory management, validation rules and web-to-case forms, according to Zoho's edition summary. Enterprise adds account-based marketing, a developer sandbox, custom buttons and functions, extended AI and field-level encryption.

## What upgrading costs

Zoho's pricing pages list four paid editions. Billed annually, Zoho lists Standard at $14, Professional at $23, Enterprise at $40 and Ultimate at $52 per user per month. Zoho's comparison table and edition PDF list the monthly-billing prices as $20, $35, $50 and $65. Only the Enterprise page's FAQ states a monthly figure cleanly ($50, alongside $40 annual). The PDF is dated 2022 and the comparison table is hard to read, so treat the Standard, Professional and Ultimate monthly prices as less certain than the annual ones. Zoho's regional pages show local currency, so your price may differ from the dollar figures here.

A rough example for a four-person team, using the listed prices and 12 months:

| Edition | Annual billing (4 users) | Monthly billing (4 users) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Standard | $672 per year | $960 per year |
| Professional | $1,104 per year | $1,680 per year |

These are our own multiplications of Zoho's list prices, before local taxes, and they assume every user needs a full regular licence. Zoho's "Choose Your Path" guide also lists lower-cost team-user licences on paid editions for non-sales staff such as marketing, finance or legal, so a mixed team may pay less.

## The "pay from the first user" rule

The free edition's three users do not carry over into a paid plan. Replies in two older threads on Zoho's community forum say that once you move to a paid edition you pay from the first user and there are no free users. We saw this wording in search-result excerpts and could not load the full threads to confirm who wrote each reply or when. Our inference is that a team of four that upgrades pays for four licences. Those replies are old forum posts, so confirm the current rule with Zoho before you plan a budget around it. It is still a useful warning: the free-to-paid jump is a step, not a gradual slope.

## Who the free edition fits

Zoho describes the free edition as built for solopreneurs, founders and small teams with one to three sales reps who rely on spreadsheets. On paper, that fits: three seats, 5,000 records and basic automation cover a small pipeline. It starts to strain when you want mass email, more than five automation rules, multiple pipelines, or a fourth user.

## How to decide

- **One to three people, a few thousand contacts, simple follow-ups:** the free edition fits, and Zoho's three-user cap is higher than HubSpot's two, per our HubSpot guide.
- **You need mass email, calling or several pipelines:** budget for Standard from the start.
- **You expect to add a fourth person soon:** price the paid edition for the whole team first, because older Zoho community replies say the per-user charge starts from user one.

Zoho's vendor comparison pages are marketing material, so treat their claims about rivals with caution and verify competitor limits on the competitors' own pages.
